Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is "financial point of view" the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 9.3. Consideration of Proposals. <omitted> (f) Notwithstanding anything in this Agreement to the contrary, during the Evaluation Period, the Company Board may evaluate a Competing Proposal <omitted> if, and only if, <omitted> (iii) the Company Board determines in good faith, after consultation with the Company’s outside legal counsel and financial advisors, that the Competing Proposal is a Superior Proposal considering the Company’s long-term strategic goals, potential regulatory hurdles, and the financial terms offered, (iv) the Company has provided written notification to Parent of the Company Board’s intention to evaluate the Competing Proposal not later than 11:59 p.m. New York time on the third Business Day following receipt of such Competing Proposal, and (v) the Company Board, after considering any adjustments proposed by Parent during the negotiation period, determines in good faith, after consultation with its outside legal counsel and a financial advisor of nationally recognized reputation, that the Competing Proposal remains a Superior Proposal, and that the failure to proceed with the Competing Proposal would be inconsistent with the fiduciary duties of the Company Board under applicable Law. Any material amendment to the financial terms or any other substantive amendment to any Competing Proposal shall require a new evaluation period, except that the reference to three Business Days shall instead be two Business Days. (Pages 89-90)\nAnswer:
